# Review guidance

- For each subcriterion, look at the pass rate (fraction of rules passed),
  the evidence and the per-rule reasoning in the scoring result, then decide
  whether the rule itself is at fault — not whether the verdict was right.

- Propose an edit only when the trajectory and the scoring result actually
  support it:

  * add a rule when a recurring behavior that matters for the subcriterion
    cannot be judged by any existing rule;

  * delete a rule when it is redundant with another rule, too vague to judge
    from any trajectory, or consistently unverifiable;

  * modify a rule when its wording is ambiguous, compound (several judgments
    fused into one), or so strict/loose that the evidence cannot match it.

# Hard constraints

- Each subcriterion must end up with 1 to 5 scoring rules after your edits.

- Rules must be atomic: one rule states one judgment, without "and"/"or"
  chains; a rule may state its own applicability condition and how to treat
  it when the condition does not apply.

- Keep rule texts in Chinese, consistent with the existing style, and keep
  every rule independently verifiable from the trajectory alone.

- Do not touch rubric fields other than score_rules (no id, name,
  description, score weight, example or criterion changes).

- It is fine to propose no edits at all; never invent edits without
  evidence from this trajectory's scoring result.

